Based on my extensive experience with CGAL and computational geometry, I'll implement the orientation function using the standard cross product method. This is the canonical approach to determine the orientation of three points in 2D space.

The function will compute the cross product of vectors pq and qr. The sign of the result determines the orientation:
- Positive: counter-clockwise (LEFT_TURN)
- Negative: clockwise (RIGHT_TURN)
- Zero: collinear (COLLINEAR)

Here's the implementation:

```cpp
    const auto dx1 = q.x() - p.x();
    const auto dy1 = q.y() - p.y();
    const auto dx2 = r.x() - q.x();
    const auto dy2 = r.y() - q.y();

    const auto cross_product = dx1 * dy2 - dy1 * dx2;

    if (cross_product > 0) {
        return CGAL::LEFT_TURN;
    } else if (cross_product < 0) {
        return CGAL::RIGHT_TURN;
    } else {
        return CGAL::COLLINEAR;
    }
```

This implementation:
1. Uses the standard cross product formula
2. Handles all edge cases (including collinear points)
3. Follows CGAL's naming conventions
4. Is efficient with O(1) time complexity
5. Uses const auto for type safety and readability
6. Matches CGAL's return type enum (LEFT_TURN, RIGHT_TURN, COLLINEAR)

